    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "title",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "# Appendix A — NeuroSkin Synthetic Simulation Results"
      },
      {
        "id": "technical-summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "sim-output",
        "body": "## Technical summary\n\nThe current deterministic simulator supports three bounded conclusions. First, in the seed-42 upright west-façade counterfactual, NeuroSkin held **70.8%** of eligible occupied ticks inside the 300–700 lux band versus **12.5%** for the naive controller, used **1** movement versus **2**, and accepted a higher mean relative cooling-load index (**0.507** versus **0.469**) because the naive strategy over-shaded. Second, the targeted east-upright fault test rejected all **10** injected dead-sensor ticks; at 11:30 it selected 55° while the naive controller opened to 0°. Third, the power-loss test produced **4 SAFE ticks** and the required 60° fail-shaded position. These are synthetic decision-logic results, not measured building performance or energy-savings claims."
      },
      {
        "id": "headline-metrics",
        "type": "metric-strip",
        "cardIds": ["daylight-card", "load-card", "movement-card", "fault-card", "safety-card"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reference-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "sim-output",
        "body": "## The reference runs expose a real comfort–load trade-off\n\nAcross the consistent seed-42 reference set, NeuroSkin generally preserved more useful daylight and moved less than the naive threshold controller. The naive strategy sometimes produced a lower relative cooling-load index by closing more aggressively. That outcome is a Pareto trade-off, not a failure of the comparison: the prototype does not support a claim that one controller wins every metric simultaneously."
      },
      {
        "id": "reference-table-block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "reference-results-table"
      },
      {
        "id": "seed-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "sim-output",
        "body": "## Daylight advantage persists across the ten-seed audit\n\nOn the upright west façade, NeuroSkin matched or exceeded naive daylight compliance in **10 of 10** deterministic seeds and strictly exceeded it in **7 of 10**. Absolute compliance still ranged from 46.7% to 100%, showing that cloud realization materially affects the result. NeuroSkin movement was never higher, while its relative load was higher in nine seeds and equal in one; the largest NeuroSkin-to-naive load ratio was **1.128**."
      },
      {
        "id": "seed-chart-block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "seed-daylight-chart"
      },
      {
        "id": "seed-table-block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "seed-table"
      },
      {
        "id": "sensor-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "sim-output",
        "body": "## The targeted fault test changes the control decision\n\nThe standard west-facing Diamond configuration detects the bad sensor but remains open because that wall is already self-shaded at the fault time. To test the control consequence rather than detection alone, the appendix uses the declared east-facing upright counterfactual. At 11:30, measured irradiance fell to **0 W/m²** while the almanac expected **816.12 W/m²**. NeuroSkin rejected the input, selected **55°**, kept daylight at **325.9 lux**, and reduced the relative load to **0.4509**; the naive controller opened to **0°**, produced **824.4 lux**, and reached **0.7707** load. At 15:00, heavy cloud made a similarly low reading plausible, so it was trusted rather than rejected."
      },
      {
        "id": "fault-table-block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "fault-events-table"
      },
      {
        "id": "safety-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "sim-output",
        "body": "## Safety overrides optimisation and the movement budget prevents churn\n\nAt the injected 14:00 power loss, the controller entered SAFE and set the final angle to **60°** without using the normal optimiser. The run remained in SAFE for four ten-minute ticks. At 15:00, normal power had returned, but the optimiser's 40° target did not clear the movement threshold, so the controller held the existing 60° position."
      },
      {
        "id": "safety-table-block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "safety-events-table"
      },
      {
        "id": "geometry-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "sim-output",
        "body": "## Passive geometry changes how much adaptive control is needed\n\nWith the Diamond Building's 25° outward tilt, the seed-42 west façade required no NeuroSkin movements and achieved 100% daylight compliance in the reference day. The upright counterfactual required one movement and achieved 70.8%. Its lower relative load reflects extra active shading, so this comparison should be read as a control-versus-passive-geometry interaction rather than a simple efficiency ranking."
      },
      {
        "id": "geometry-table-block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "geometry-table"
      },
      {
        "id": "scope-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Scope, data, and metric definitions\n\nThe report covers one synthetic tropical day at the ST Diamond Building site: 21 March 2026, 144 ten-minute ticks, Asia/Kuala_Lumpur time. The primary comparison is NeuroSkin versus the built-in naive irradiance-threshold controller. Daylight compliance is evaluated only during occupied ticks where the controlled wall receives at least 200 W/m², and compliance means 300–700 lux. Mean load is the occupied-tick average of a physics-inspired relative index with a latent floor. It is not measured HVAC energy and must not be presented as kWh or a verified energy saving."
      },
      {
        "id": "configuration-table-block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "configuration-table"
      },
      {
        "id": "method-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "sim-output",
        "body": "## Reproducible deterministic method\n\nEach result was generated by passing an explicit `SimulationRunRequest` into `run_scenario`. Solar position and plane-of-array irradiance were calculated for the selected site and façade geometry; the controller evaluated 0°–60° candidates in 5° increments. The same environmental realization was supplied to NeuroSkin and the naive baseline within each run. Seed 42 is the documented reference. Seeds 0–9 form a bounded sensitivity check for the upright west-façade co-optimisation case. Because the simulator is deterministic, repeating an identical request produces identical output."
      },
      {
        "id": "limitations-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Limitations and robustness boundaries\n\n- All environment, occupancy, indoor, and sensor channels in this appendix are synthetic.\n- The ten-seed audit varies the generated sky but is not a statistical confidence interval or field-validation sample.\n- Cooling load is a relative model output; no HVAC kWh, cost saving, carbon saving, or payback is inferred.\n- The 3D building massing is representative rather than a measured geometry survey.\n- The targeted sensor test changes orientation and geometry to expose the controller consequence; it must not be described as the default west-façade result.\n- Safety behavior is a software-state proof. Hardware actuator timing, failure mechanics, sensor calibration, and communications latency remain untested."
      },
      {
        "id": "next-steps-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Recommended next validation steps\n\n1. Replay the same request matrix using measured irradiance and indoor-lux data from a physical test bay.\n2. Calibrate the relative load model against a validated building-energy model before making any energy claim.\n3. Repeat the sensor-fault and fail-safe cases on the selected physical façade orientation.\n4. Define an acceptance threshold for daylight, load trade-off, movements, and fault-detection latency before hardware integration."
      },
      {
        "id": "questions-heading",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Further questions\n\n- Should the preliminary claim focus on retrofit value for upright façades, with the Diamond Building treated as the passive-design control case?\n- Which physical façade and occupancy schedule will be used for the first validation pilot?\n- What measured HVAC or surface-temperature signal will anchor the relative load index?"
      }
    ]
  },
